\begin{abstract}
\noindent
We present Axon--FP, a unified real-time astrophysical simulation engine designed for galactic-scale modeling of stellar evolution, habitability, abiogenesis probability, and civilizational percolation dynamics. The system integrates Gaia DR3 and APOGEE-2 astrometric catalogs, MESA/MIST stellar evolution tracks, HEALPix tessellation, dynamic octree partitioning, and a deterministic Rust/OpenCL/CUDA compute pipeline. Stress testing under synthetic loads exceeding $10.2\times10^9$ sources demonstrates full numerical stability across $13.8\,$Gyr of simulated cosmic time. Axon--FP is released as an open computational framework with full reproducibility across heterogeneous hardware.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{methods: numerical --- galaxies: evolution --- astrobiology --- civilizations --- software: simulations}

\section{Introduction}
Large-scale astrophysical simulations typically isolate stellar evolution, habitability modeling, and civilizational dynamics into separate pipelines. Axon--FP provides a unified architecture capable of real-time computation across billions of stellar objects, integrating astrophysical, biological, and sociotechnical processes into a single deterministic engine.

\section{Software Overview}
Axon--FP is implemented in Rust with Rayon concurrency, OpenCL/CUDA compute shaders, and WebGPU rendering. The engine supports deterministic floating-point execution, fixed PRNG seeding, and hardware-agnostic reproducibility across NVIDIA, AMD, Intel, and Apple Silicon GPUs.

\section{Data Sources}
\subsection{Observational Anchors}
Gaia DR3 and APOGEE-2 provide astrometric and spectroscopic ground truth for $1.5\times10^9$ stars. MESA/MIST stellar evolution tracks supply validated temporal behavior across the H-R diagram.

\subsection{Synthetic Validation}
Bayesian imputation and MCMC inference fill missing parameters, cross-tested against mock catalogs generated from IllustrisTNG cosmological simulations.

